11-14T08:14:53Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#43574567599", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Jan of Głogów was a notable Polish polyhistor at the turn of the Middle Ages and Renaissance—a philosopher, geographer and astronomer at the University of Krakow.", "newValue": "Jan of Głogów (Latin: Johannes Glogoviensis), born in Głogów in 1445 and dying in Krakow on February 11, 1507, was a towering figure in the intellectual life of Central Europe at the cusp of the Middle Ages and the Renaissance. A distinguished polymath, he spent over four decades as a professor at the University of Krakow (now Jagiellonian University), where his expertise spanned philosophy, astronomy, mathematics, and geography. His work exemplified the era's blend of rigorous scholasticism with a burgeoning interest in empirical observation and classical humanism, establishing Krakow as a significant center for scientific inquiry.

During his extensive tenure at Krakow, Jan of Głogów authored numerous influential treatises. In philosophy, he produced commentaries on Aristotle's works, particularly on logic and natural philosophy, which became standard texts. His astronomical contributions included detailed tables and ephemerides, crucial for navigation and astrological prognostication, alongside his notable prediction of the comet of 1490. Furthermore, his geographical writings, which incorporated Ptolomeic principles and contemporary observations, significantly advanced cartography and regional understanding, making him a pivotal figure in the transmission of scientific knowledge across the region.

Jan's profound legacy is most notably reflected in his role as a mentor to a generation of scholars, most famously Nicolaus Copernicus, who studied under him at Krakow from 1491 to 1495. His emphasis on mathematical rigor, observational astronomy, and critical analysis of classical texts undoubtedly laid a foundational intellectual groundwork for Copernicus's revolutionary heliocentric theory. Through his teaching and prolific writings, Jan of Głogów effectively transmitted and expanded the scientific and philosophical heritage of both antiquity and the Islamic Golden Age, fostering an environment of intellectual curiosity that resonated far beyond his lifetime."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-14T08:14:53Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#43574567599", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1445, Głogów"}, {"title": "Ongoing philosophical inquiry and intellectual debate", "type": "Idea", "year": "1445, Głogów"}], "newValue": ["The flourishing intellectual environment of the University of Krakow in the late 15th century fostered polymathic scholarship.", "The ongoing transmission of classical Greek and Arabic scientific texts through medieval scholasticism provided a rich foundation for his studies.", "The emergence of Renaissance humanism encouraged a broader inquiry beyond traditional theological confines, influencing his diverse interests."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-14T08:14:53Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#43574567599", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Contributed philosophical ideas to ongoing intellectual discourse", "type": "Idea", "year": "1507-02-11"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1507-02-11"}], "newValue": ["He significantly advanced astronomical and geographical knowledg